<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Hira</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Hira was an ancient city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, south of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, capital of the Arab Lakhmid kings from the late 3rd century until 602. A centre of Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and early Arabic poetry, it served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> as a frontier base facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the desert tribes. It surrendered to the Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> in 633 and declined after Kufa was founded nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-hira.jpg" alt="Al-Hira" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamal-ud-din_Bihzad_-_Construction_of_the_fort_of_Kharnaq.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
